# rm — real-world examples

## Remove a directory and everything under it

```sh
rm -rf path/to/the/target/
```

## Ignore nonexistent files

```sh
# -f: no error if the file is already gone (good for scripts)
rm -f path/to/the/target
```

## Remove a file by inode

```sh
# For files with unprintable/hostile names: find the inode, then delete it
ls -li /tmp/
find /tmp/ -inum 6666 -exec rm -i '{}' \;
```

## Safety habits

```sh
# Prompt before every removal
rm -i important.conf

# Prompt once when deleting many files (>3) or recursively
rm -I *.log

# Stop option parsing — deletes a file literally named "-rf"
rm -- -rf

# Dry-run first with ls: see exactly what the glob matches
ls /srv/media/tmp/*.partial
rm /srv/media/tmp/*.partial
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# Clear old transcode temp files on mediaserver
rm -rf /var/tmp/plex-transcode/*

# Remove logs older than 30 days (find does the matching, safer than a glob)
find /home/david/logs -name '*.log' -mtime +30 -exec rm {} \;

# Empty a build directory but keep the directory itself
rm -rf ~/builds/project/*
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-r` | Recursive (directories) |
| `-f` | Force: no prompts, no error on missing files |
| `-i` | Prompt before each removal |
| `-I` | Prompt once for many/recursive removals |
| `-v` | Print each file as it's removed |
| `--` | End of options (protects odd filenames) |
